 "collected": "2026-09-18 from bw1100, by running tools/read_dcu_confirmatory_medians.py over the campaign workspaces under /home/testuser01/oci-dcu-runs inside the DTK image, which is where they are readable. The tool owns exactly the fields it lists in generated_fields, which it derives from its own output rather than declaring beside it, and rewrites them from sealed evidence. The five it does not own -- what, collected, row_selection, receipt_paths and footprint_sweep -- are written by hand, so renewing this file means running the tool and keeping those five. Saying which is which matters, because this field has misdescribed how the file was made on three successive attempts. It claimed the tool rewrote the whole document when the tool emitted ten keys of fifteen, so running it with --out would have deleted five. Before that it named an extractor that existed in no commit and no file. Before that it named two sweeps that cover eight of this table's twenty-seven tasks.",
 "row_selection": "Three tasks qualified more than once, so thirty qualified runs produce twenty-seven rows and the choice of run had to be made. Each row is the task's FIRST qualified run by workspace timestamp. The rule is stated because it is not neutral: gelu_tanh's later run reads better than the one kept, so taking the first is the conservative direction rather than the flattering one. The rows span three Compiler revisions. campaign_revisions covers every campaign on this host, qualified or not -- sixty-seven of them across twenty-nine tasks and seven revisions -- so four revisions in it are used by no row: three of those ran before every row, on campaigns of rmsnorm that qualified nothing, and one ran after every row. An earlier version of this field said there was one such revision and gave 'ran later than every row' as the reason, which is backwards for three of the four. A record citing runs this table has no row for -- F-2026-09-18-003 cites the runs at two, four and six turns -- takes its revisions from campaign_revisions.",
 "receipt_paths": "Each row's `receipt` is a path inside its workspace's content-addressed object store, which is why it looks like a digest. AGENTS.md says findings cite workspaces and runs by path and index and do not copy digests, because the ledger's own hash chain settles byte identity. No digest of a finding, a release or an artifact is recorded here to prove identity, so that rule is not engaged. The alternative AGENTS.md names -- path and index -- would be the run id plus the event file plus the object's role, which is how the tool finds the receipt in the first place; it is three lookups a reader must perform by hand, and two of the three are not stable under re-sealing, while the object path is the store's own name for that byte sequence and resolves in one step. Nothing re-hashes these, and no test asserts one matches a payload.",
